Got it — going deep. Since you didn't weigh in on the other two, I'm going with these defaults (say the word if you want it flipped):
- **No Docker** in the main path — deployment examples use GitHub Pages, TestPyPI, and plain SSH-to-server, which is honest and doesn't require your Docker course as a prerequisite. I'll drop one line pointing to the Docker course for anyone who wants a containerized deploy variant, but won't build it out.
- **Tutorial chapters only** — this reads more like a hands-on cert-prep course than a narrative one, so blog-style felt off. Easy to add a couple of blog posts later if you want a "why I built the pipeline this way" reflection piece.

Here's the full skeleton — **58 chapters across 9 sections**. Python is the default example language throughout (pytest, pip, flake8/ruff), with non-Python moments called out where the topic itself is language-agnostic (YAML syntax, runner internals, etc.).

